      "sourceTextSha256": "48e3dcb3f980f1c87efa651e76295b813aeed3262d797deae99d9ce607b0d350",
      "text": "South Shetland Islands",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261107-mw6jtuzg-igwdq2wieu0es0sd2yppef",
        "model": "gpt-6.1-sol"
      }
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:fb5db666cae6efb7",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "7b6a860a17caa12862104cfe288fd519c59e5973f5a5ba5a125bd86b1708b5a5",
      "text": "Wind and fog often sweep over the volcanic South Shetland Islands. Mosses, lichens and flowering grasses grow here, and the islands are home to gentoo and chinstrap penguins and southern giant petrels.\n\nOne option is Deception Island, where the ship enters a flooded caldera through the narrow opening known as Neptune’s Bellows. Near the abandoned whaling station, you can see thousands of Cape petrels, as well as gulls, brown and south polar skuas, and Antarctic terns. A hike across the volcanic terrain may be possible. Another option is to visit the area around Half Moon Island, where chinstrap penguins and Weddell seals can be found on the shore near Argentina’s Cámara Base. Either option may replace the other. Conditions in the Drake Passage determine exactly when we depart for the return crossing.",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261107-mw6jtuzg-igwdq2wieu0es0sd2yppef",
        "model": "gpt-6.1-sol"
      }
    },
